WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
<v Narrator>Psychological safety makes it more likely that people raise problems early.

2
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:10.150
It does not replace formal routes, and it does not make anyone an investigator.

3
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.100
The respectful workplace reading separates three layers.

4
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:18.050
Federal and Minnesota law set the floor for illegal harassment and discrimination.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.800
State policy sets a higher standard for daily conduct.

6
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:26.750
Policy 1436 covers unwelcome conduct tied to a protected class, including offensive

7
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.100
jokes, slurs, deliberately misgendering someone, and deliberately mispronouncing

8
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:31.450
their name.

9
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.200
Policy 1432 covers disrespect that is not tied to a protected class, such as

10
00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.350
shouting, bullying or demeaning treatment.

11
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:41.500
Here is the practical point.

12
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:47.250
You do not have to decide which layer something fits before you raise it.

13
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:53.000
Sorting that out belongs to Human Resources and the DHS Equal Opportunity and Access

14
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:54.350
Division.

15
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:59.100
<v Narrator>If you see disrespect or harassment happen, you have options.

16
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:05.250
Right To Be, a national nonprofit, teaches five of them, known as the five Ds.

17
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.600
Distract: interrupt the moment, perhaps with a work question or a short break.

18
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:16.350
Delegate: bring in someone with more authority, such as a supervisor or a meeting

19
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:17.700
chair.

20
00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:21.050
Document: make a factual note of what happened.

21
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:27.200
Delay: check in afterward, say it was not okay, and ask what the person needs.

22
00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:33.350
Direct: name the behavior briefly, but only when it is safe and likely to help.

23
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:40.300
With any note you make, ask the targeted person what they want done with it, and do

24
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:46.450
not share it without their permission, unless you are a supervisor with a duty to

25
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.800
report.

26
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.750
Delay is often the most underused option.

27
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:55.300
It leaves the targeted person in control of what happens next.

28
00:01:56.050 --> 00:02:00.450
<v Narrator>If a colleague brings you a concern, listen and thank them.

29
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.800
Do not judge whether it is serious enough.

30
00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:07.950
Be honest about what you can and cannot keep confidential.

31
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:09.700
Then share the routes.

32
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.050
A supervisor.

33
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.400
Human Resources.

34
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.950
The Equal Opportunity and Access Division.

35
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.300
A union representative.

36
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.050
The Employee Assistance Program.

37
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:23.800
And agencies outside DHS, such as the Minnesota Department of Human Rights or the

38
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.950
federal Equal Employment Opportunity Commission.

39
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:30.900
Do not interview other people or try to work out what happened.

40
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.850
Route the concern; do not investigate it.

41
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.600
And watch for retaliation.

42
00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:40.950
No one may be punished for reporting, for opposing harassment or discrimination, or

43
00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.500
for taking part in an investigation.

44
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:49.250
Retaliation can be obvious, like a worse assignment, or subtle, like being left out

45
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.600
of meetings.

46
00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:56.150
<v Narrator>If you are a manager or supervisor, there is a specific duty.

47
00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:59.900
Under Policy 1436, managers and supervisors immediately report protected-class

48
00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:04.450
allegations or incidents to Human Resources or the agency Affirmative Action

49
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.600
Officer, and follow agency procedures.

50
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:11.550
DHS policy directs supervisors to refer these complaints to the Equal Opportunity

51
00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.900
and Access Division, and not to investigate them.

52
00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:20.250
That means no interviewing witnesses, no asking the person to prove what happened,

53
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.600
no confronting the person accused, and no mediating.

54
00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:26.950
You cannot agree to keep protected-class harassment secret.

55
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:33.100
And a team conversation, an apology or an attempt at repair can never delay that

56
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:34.450
report.

57
00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.200
Repair may matter later.

58
00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.350
It is never a gate.

59
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.100
<v Narrator>Back to everyday team habits.

60
00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.050
Edmondson's toolkit for leaders has three parts.

61
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:49.800
Set the stage by framing the work as uncertain and tying it to purpose.

62
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.150
Invite participation through humility, inquiry and structured forums.

63
00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.700
Respond productively by appreciating input, removing the stigma from failure, and

64
00:03:57.850 --> 00:03:59.450
still sanctioning clear violations.

65
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:06.000
In practice, “Any questions?” at the end of a meeting is often too broad and too

66
00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:07.350
late.

67
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:12.700
A useful invitation names what is being decided, what is still uncertain, who

68
00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.850
decides, and how people can respond before the choice closes.

69
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.600
One reading adds a simple habit: before deciding, ask for the strongest case against

70
00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:23.950
your own idea.

71
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.500
Her book adds one more point.

72
00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:32.250
A leader's reaction to the first person who speaks up shapes whether anyone else

73
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.600
will.

74
00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.750
<v Narrator>An invitation is only a beginning.

75
00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.900
People watch what happens next.

76
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.450
Did the question get an answer?

77
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.400
Did the answer address what was asked?

78
00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.950
One reading suggests a regular habit.

79
00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:52.300
Say what changed because someone spoke up, and if nothing did, say why.

80
00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:56.050
You do not have to agree with every suggestion.

81
00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:01.000
What helps is a useful account of how the question was considered.

82
00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:03.950
The same reading adds: watch the grind.

83
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.700
Constant urgency silences people.

84
00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:09.050
Protect time for thinking, and for saying no.

85
00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:14.000
Thank people who raise concerns, in a way that respects their preferences.

86
00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:16.150
Public thanks may be welcome.

87
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:20.300
It should never expose someone who raised a concern privately.

88
00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.850
<v Narrator>Finally, be careful how you judge progress.

89
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:28.400
Fewer questions could mean clearer instructions, less opportunity, or reluctance to

90
00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:29.750
ask.

91
00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:35.500
More concerns could mean the route works better, or that a new problem appeared.

92
00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:38.050
A count alone cannot settle it.

93
00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.800
Avoid ranking people by how often they speak, and avoid labels for a quiet

94
00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:45.150
colleague.

95
00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:46.900
Describe what actually happened.

96
00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:48.650
Which questions got answers.

97
00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.400
Which are still open.

98
00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.550
What one person told you, kept to its own scope.

99
00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.100
What nobody has said stays unknown.

100
00:05:57.250 --> 00:06:02.450
That kind of honest, mixed account gives the next conversation something real to

101
00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:03.800
build on.

